7592186045417] [17592186416480 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416480 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416480 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186416448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416480 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8cda2f1-5552-5357-82cc-f1ab61cac36e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95266" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $2$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $5\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5650e0d2-585c-5516-892d-5eacef061cb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416481 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 2}\\times {A}_{2\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is a $5\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 5$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 5}\\times {A}_{5\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97ca7c6b-3d02-595e-9b24-0e63b350342b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416482 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}17-\\lambda  & -8 \\\\ -8 & 17-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (17-\\lambda )(17-\\lambda )-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +289-64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-34\\lambda +225 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -25) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =25,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{25}=5 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-139508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ab4ca253-4533-55ea-9cf5-518c8864d1f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416483 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (9-\\lambda )(9-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c791e385-b793-500d-9a77-3e468ca25a09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416484 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFirst, we calculate ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the characteristic equation of ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{A}^{T}\\,A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda -5\\lambda +40-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the singular values of $A$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{1} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{1}}=\\sqrt{9}=3 \\\\ {\\sigma }_{2} & =\\sqrt{{\\lambda }_{2}}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416480 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95261" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $4\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The singular values of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ are non-negative.\n- Any $3\\times 4$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e69dd032-aa5c-5891-a7fc-97b84703e88a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416485 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is true. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $4\\times 4$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{4\\times 3}\\times {A}_{3\\times 4}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{4\\times 4}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186416479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- Any $6\\times 3$ matrix $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n- The matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ has one negative singular value.\n- Any $4\\times 5$ matrix $A$ has at most $4$ distinct singular values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fef86daf-091a-52fa-bcf0-1d2b609bb348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416486 #edb/ref 1074 "The singular values of the matrix $A$ are the square roots of the eigenvalues of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ is a $6\\times 3$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $3\\times 6$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $3\\times 3$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{3\\times 6}\\times {A}_{6\\times 3}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{3\\times 3}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $3$ distinct singular values.\n\n- Statement II is false. All singular values of any matrix are non-negative. So, $A$ can't have a negative singular value.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is a $4\\times 5$ matrix and ${A}^{T}$ is a $5\\times 4$ matrix, ${A}^{T}\\,A$ must be a $5\\times 5$ matrix: \n\n$$\n{{A}^{T}}_{5\\times 4}\\times {A}_{4\\times 5}={{A}^{T}\\,A}_{5\\times 5}\n$$\n\nHence, $A$ has at most $5$ distinct singular values.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
